The matrix $ Q $ has orthonormal columns, meaning $ Q^T Q = I_n $ (where $ I_n $ is the $ n \times n $ identity matrix). However, $ QQ^T $ is an $ m \times m $ matrix. For $ QQ^T $ to equal the $ m \times m $ identity matrix $ I_m $, $ Q $ would need to be square (i.e., $ m = n $), which is not the case here. Since $ m > n $, $ QQ^T $ projects vectors onto the column space of $ Q $, which is a proper subspace of $ \mathbb{R}^m $. Thus, $ QQ^T \neq I_m $.

**Final Answer**
\boxed{False}

